>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>Control Statements in Java<<<<<<<<<<<<<
Control statements are used to control the flow of execution of a program.

1. if statement – Executes a block when the condition is true.
int a = 10;
if (a > 5) {
    System.out.println("Greater");
}


2. if-else statement – Executes one block if true, otherwise another block.
int a = 5;
if (a > 0)
    System.out.println("Positive");
else
    System.out.println("Negative");


3. else-if ladder – Checks multiple conditions.
int n = 0;
if (n > 0)
    System.out.println("Positive");
else if (n < 0)
    System.out.println("Negative");
else
    System.out.println("Zero");


4. switch statement – Selects one option from multiple cases.
int day = 1;
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Monday"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tuesday"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Invalid");
}


5. for loop – Repeats a block a fixed number of times.
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    System.out.println(i);


6. while loop – Repeats while the condition is true.
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}


7. do-while loop – Executes at least once before checking the condition.
int i = 1;
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while (i <= 5);


8. break statement – Terminates the loop immediately.
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;
    System.out.println(i);
}


9. continue statement – Skips the current iteration.
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;
    System.out.println(i);
}

